WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:04.044
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll use

00:00:04.044 --> 00:00:06.917
String positions and ranges, track nested

00:00:06.917 --> 00:00:10.140
delimiters—paired boundary symbols—and write a

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.364
method that returns the right section of text.

00:00:15.791 --> 00:00:19.094
These text-processing skills help applications

00:00:19.094 --> 00:00:22.541
extract usable information from notes and files,

00:00:22.541 --> 00:00:25.629
while a clear method contract—the rules for

00:00:25.629 --> 00:00:29.004
accepted inputs and returned results—lets other

00:00:29.004 --> 00:00:32.163
code interpret the result and test difficult

00:00:32.163 --> 00:00:32.666
inputs.

00:00:34.049 --> 00:00:37.225
A workshop coordinator stores color notes inside

00:00:37.225 --> 00:00:38.284
square brackets.

00:00:38.284 --> 00:00:41.262
Some notes contain another bracketed section,

00:00:41.262 --> 00:00:43.711
which must stay in the selected text.

00:00:43.711 --> 00:00:46.623
We'll build a helper that reads the note one

00:00:46.623 --> 00:00:49.667
position at a time, counts openings that still

00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:52.512
need a close, and selects the inside of the

00:00:52.512 --> 00:00:54.299
first completed outer pair.

00:00:55.753 --> 00:00:58.694
The caller should receive the entire marked

00:00:58.694 --> 00:01:01.773
section with only its outer brackets removed.

00:01:01.773 --> 00:01:03.893
Later groups won't be included.

00:01:03.893 --> 00:01:07.040
An empty pair or a note with no completed pair

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.024
will produce an empty String.

00:01:09.024 --> 00:01:12.307
We'll learn the position rules first, then build

00:01:12.307 --> 00:01:14.428
and test that selection method.

00:01:14.742 --> 00:01:17.716
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:01:17.716 --> 00:01:20.966
Main.java and try the two String operations our

00:01:20.966 --> 00:01:22.142
helper will need.

00:01:26.573 --> 00:01:29.730
Main is the class holding our program, and its

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.749
main method is where the application starts.

00:01:32.749 --> 00:01:35.220
We'll put a short marked word there.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.896
String positions start at zero: in this

00:01:37.896 --> 00:01:41.053
six-character input, the opening bracket is at

00:01:41.053 --> 00:01:43.798
zero and the closing bracket is at five.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:55.373
To recognize a boundary, the scan needs one

00:01:55.373 --> 00:01:57.880
character rather than a whole String.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.590
The charAt method selects the value at a

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:01.200
position.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.181
We'll select position zero and store it in a

00:02:04.181 --> 00:02:06.010
char variable named symbol.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:09.194
Single quotes write a character literal; double

00:02:09.194 --> 00:02:10.685
quotes write a String.

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:23.419
Symbol contains the opening bracket, so its

00:02:23.419 --> 00:02:25.723
character comparison should be true.

00:02:25.723 --> 00:02:28.731
Equality compares these char values; it isn't a

00:02:28.731 --> 00:02:31.546
replacement for equals when comparing String

00:02:31.546 --> 00:02:32.058
content.

00:02:32.058 --> 00:02:34.874
Next we need the text between two positions.

00:02:34.874 --> 00:02:37.754
Substring includes its start and excludes its

00:02:37.754 --> 00:02:40.698
end, so the range from one to five selects the

00:02:40.698 --> 00:02:42.042
four letters of blue.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:00.348
The second substring call has equal boundaries.

00:03:00.348 --> 00:03:03.798
No position belongs to that range, so it returns

00:03:03.798 --> 00:03:04.948
an empty String.

00:03:04.948 --> 00:03:08.039
The labels and angle brackets come from the

00:03:08.039 --> 00:03:10.698
caller and make both results visible.

00:03:10.698 --> 00:03:13.070
Let's click Run and compare them.

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:21.797
The output shows the bracket, true, the selected

00:03:21.797 --> 00:03:23.907
word, and an empty labeled result.

00:03:23.907 --> 00:03:26.637
Neither operation changed the source String.

00:03:26.637 --> 00:03:29.553
These ordinary letters and brackets each occupy

00:03:29.553 --> 00:03:32.407
one position; Java actually counts UTF-sixteen

00:03:32.407 --> 00:03:35.323
code units, so some displayed symbols need more

00:03:35.323 --> 00:03:36.440
than one position.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:39.418
We'll stay with these simple characters while we

00:03:39.418 --> 00:03:40.969
build the delimiter scan.

00:03:41.018 --> 00:03:43.854
We can select a range now, but a nested note has

00:03:43.854 --> 00:03:45.626
more than one closing bracket.

00:03:45.626 --> 00:03:48.403
Stopping at the first close would cut the outer

00:03:48.403 --> 00:03:49.230
section short.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:52.007
Let's replace the small trial with TextTools, a

00:03:52.007 --> 00:03:54.783
class containing our reusable selection method.

00:03:54.783 --> 00:03:57.501
It accepts a String, including empty text, and

00:03:57.501 --> 00:03:59.332
returns a String to its caller.

00:03:59.332 --> 00:04:01.873
We won't pass null, the value that means no

00:04:01.873 --> 00:04:03.468
String object was supplied.

00:04:14.112 --> 00:04:17.206
Start remembers where the outer group's content

00:04:17.206 --> 00:04:17.666
begins.

00:04:17.666 --> 00:04:20.826
Minus one means that we haven't found an opening

00:04:20.826 --> 00:04:21.089
yet.

00:04:21.089 --> 00:04:24.051
Depth counts openings that still need closing

00:04:24.051 --> 00:04:24.643
brackets.

00:04:24.643 --> 00:04:27.737
We'll move through valid positions, starting at

00:04:27.737 --> 00:04:30.567
zero and stopping before the String length,

00:04:30.567 --> 00:04:32.937
selecting one char at each position.

00:04:44.823 --> 00:04:47.951
At an opening bracket, depth tells us whether

00:04:47.951 --> 00:04:51.009
this is a new outer group or a smaller group

00:04:51.009 --> 00:04:51.704
inside it.

00:04:51.704 --> 00:04:55.040
Only an opening seen at depth zero should record

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:55.874
a new start.

00:04:55.874 --> 00:04:58.793
We store the next position so the eventual

00:04:58.793 --> 00:05:01.573
substring leaves that outer bracket out.

00:05:14.933 --> 00:05:17.801
Every opening increases depth, but an inner

00:05:17.801 --> 00:05:19.602
opening leaves start alone.

00:05:19.602 --> 00:05:22.803
For the short note with m, an inner bracketed n,

00:05:22.803 --> 00:05:25.804
and p, start stays one while depth rises from

00:05:25.804 --> 00:05:26.538
one to two.

00:05:26.538 --> 00:05:29.406
A closing bracket should count only when an

00:05:29.406 --> 00:05:32.340
opening is active; that guard also ignores a

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:35.408
stray closing bracket before any group begins.

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:47.597
A closing bracket decreases depth.

00:05:47.597 --> 00:05:50.797
The inner close in our short note changes two to

00:05:50.797 --> 00:05:52.863
one, so scanning must continue.

00:05:52.863 --> 00:05:55.597
Only the outer close changes one to zero.

00:05:55.597 --> 00:05:58.597
At that moment we know both range boundaries:

00:05:58.597 --> 00:06:01.397
the recorded start and the current closing

00:06:01.397 --> 00:06:03.730
position, which substring excludes.

00:06:17.834 --> 00:06:20.978
This early return finishes the entire method,

00:06:20.978 --> 00:06:22.305
including its loop.

00:06:22.305 --> 00:06:25.658
Java won't perform another loop update or scan a

00:06:25.658 --> 00:06:26.496
later group.

00:06:26.496 --> 00:06:29.640
But an input might end before any outer group

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:30.339
completes.

00:06:30.339 --> 00:06:33.692
Outside the complete loop, we'll return an empty

00:06:33.692 --> 00:06:35.159
String for that case.

00:06:38.402 --> 00:06:41.016
The helper now has both return paths.

00:06:41.016 --> 00:06:44.194
It doesn't print or change the original text;

00:06:44.194 --> 00:06:46.737
display remains the caller's choice.

00:06:46.737 --> 00:06:49.986
Let's call it with our short nested note so we

00:06:49.986 --> 00:06:53.024
can trace the result before trying a longer

00:06:53.024 --> 00:06:53.377
note.

00:07:08.064 --> 00:07:10.790
The opening at zero records start one.

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:14.017
The inner opening at two raises depth to two,

00:07:14.017 --> 00:07:17.030
and its close at four lowers depth to one.

00:07:17.030 --> 00:07:20.042
The close at six finally makes depth zero.

00:07:20.042 --> 00:07:23.414
The selected positions one through five contain

00:07:23.414 --> 00:07:26.354
m, the complete inner bracketed n, and p.

00:07:26.354 --> 00:07:27.789
Let's run that call.

00:07:34.083 --> 00:07:36.757
The caller prints m, bracketed n, and p.

00:07:36.757 --> 00:07:39.765
The inner pair survived because it was inside

00:07:39.765 --> 00:07:41.035
the selected range.

00:07:41.035 --> 00:07:43.843
Start stayed at the outer content boundary

00:07:43.843 --> 00:07:46.851
throughout the scan, and only the outer close

00:07:46.851 --> 00:07:48.121
allowed the return.

00:07:48.121 --> 00:07:50.728
Now let's examine the rule for empty or

00:07:50.728 --> 00:07:53.870
incomplete input before using the helper on the

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:55.608
coordinator's longer note.

00:07:55.752 --> 00:07:58.855
We'll keep the helper unchanged and replace the

00:07:58.855 --> 00:08:00.968
caller with four separate tests.

00:08:00.968 --> 00:08:03.940
The first input has two completed groups; the

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:06.977
method should return only the first group's a.

00:08:06.977 --> 00:08:10.014
An empty pair should return empty immediately.

00:08:10.014 --> 00:08:13.118
Plain text and an unfinished group should reach

00:08:13.118 --> 00:08:15.627
the final empty return after the loop.

00:08:16.596 --> 00:08:19.079
The same selection rule is in place.

00:08:19.079 --> 00:08:21.976
Each test now needs its own call, and each

00:08:21.976 --> 00:08:24.942
caller will print angle brackets around the

00:08:24.942 --> 00:08:25.425
result.

00:08:25.425 --> 00:08:28.322
This makes an empty return visible without

00:08:28.322 --> 00:08:31.288
pretending that the helper printed anything

00:08:31.288 --> 00:08:31.771
itself.

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:36.024
Let's run the four calls in their displayed

00:09:36.024 --> 00:09:39.311
order: two groups, an empty group, no group, and

00:09:39.311 --> 00:09:40.680
an unfinished group.

00:09:46.384 --> 00:09:48.265
Only the first line contains a.

00:09:48.265 --> 00:09:50.813
That early return skipped the later group.

00:09:50.813 --> 00:09:53.664
The next three lines all contain empty results,

00:09:53.664 --> 00:09:56.030
but their causes differ: the empty pair

00:09:56.030 --> 00:09:58.881
completed a zero-length range, while plain text

00:09:58.881 --> 00:10:01.429
and the unfinished group reached the final

00:10:01.429 --> 00:10:01.854
return.

00:10:01.854 --> 00:10:04.462
An empty result alone doesn't tell a caller

00:10:04.462 --> 00:10:07.374
which cause occurred; that limitation belongs in

00:10:07.374 --> 00:10:08.709
the method's contract.

00:10:08.887 --> 00:10:11.857
The boundary checks match the stated rules.

00:10:11.857 --> 00:10:14.897
We'll keep the helper and replace those four

00:10:14.897 --> 00:10:18.144
calls with the coordinator's longer color note.

00:10:18.144 --> 00:10:21.252
This time, use the depth rule yourself before

00:10:21.252 --> 00:10:22.012
running it.

00:10:22.862 --> 00:10:24.616
The helper is unchanged.

00:10:24.616 --> 00:10:27.906
The new input contains an outer color section

00:10:27.906 --> 00:10:30.099
with an inner bracketed color.

00:10:30.099 --> 00:10:33.023
The caller will print exactly the String

00:10:33.023 --> 00:10:36.312
returned by the method, with no extra labels.

00:10:55.637 --> 00:10:58.467
Pause here and predict the printed text.

00:10:58.467 --> 00:11:01.721
Which opening records start, and which closing

00:11:01.721 --> 00:11:04.551
bracket first brings depth back to zero?

00:11:04.551 --> 00:11:07.947
Decide which brackets belong inside the selected

00:11:07.947 --> 00:11:09.787
range before you continue.

00:11:14.972 --> 00:11:18.281
Let's click Run and compare your prediction with

00:11:18.281 --> 00:11:19.522
the actual result.

00:11:25.995 --> 00:11:28.982
The output is red, bracketed blue, then green.

00:11:28.982 --> 00:11:31.514
The outer opening records start at six.

00:11:31.514 --> 00:11:34.631
The inner pair raises depth to two and lowers it

00:11:34.631 --> 00:11:37.229
to one, so it doesn't end the selection.

00:11:37.229 --> 00:11:40.281
The outer close at twenty brings depth to zero,

00:11:40.281 --> 00:11:43.398
and the range from six to twenty keeps the inner

00:11:43.398 --> 00:11:45.995
brackets while excluding the outer pair.

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:48.874
We built a reusable method that extracts the

00:11:48.874 --> 00:11:51.217
coordinator's complete marked section.

00:11:51.217 --> 00:11:54.116
String positions let us inspect characters, the

00:11:54.116 --> 00:11:56.768
depth counter identified the matching outer

00:11:56.768 --> 00:11:59.420
boundary, substring selected the range, and

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:02.196
early return stopped work once the answer was

00:12:02.196 --> 00:12:02.566
known.

00:12:02.566 --> 00:12:05.465
The caller handled output, while boundary tests

00:12:05.465 --> 00:12:07.685
made the empty-result rule explicit.

00:12:07.867 --> 00:12:11.119
For a transfer check, imagine that the notes use

00:12:11.119 --> 00:12:13.762
parentheses instead of square brackets.

00:12:13.762 --> 00:12:16.947
Which comparisons would change, and which parts

00:12:16.947 --> 00:12:19.860
of the range and depth logic would stay the

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:20.199
same?

00:12:20.199 --> 00:12:23.451
Explain how you would test both a nested section

00:12:23.451 --> 00:12:24.942
and an unfinished one.
